INSERT INTO repo_courses (course_id, title, domain, discipline, description, objectives, learning_outcomes, course_type, level, status) VALUES ('COURSE_031', 'Foundation Engineering', 'Civil Engineering', 'Civil Engineering', 'Foundation Engineering applies soil mechanics and site-characterisation principles to the analysis and design of shallow and deep foundations. It covers subsurface investigation, soil parameters, bearing capacity, settlement, foundation-system selection, pile behaviour, construction considerations, and the interaction between ground conditions, structural loads, groundwater, and foundation performance.', 'Develop competence in interpreting site investigation data, selecting representative soil parameters, assessing bearing capacity and settlement, choosing foundation systems, and accounting for construction and ground-condition uncertainties in design.', 'Plan and interpret subsurface investigations; determine design parameters from field and laboratory evidence; analyse shallow-foundation bearing capacity and settlement; evaluate pile and deep-foundation behaviour; select appropriate foundation systems; and identify construction and ground-related risks affecting foundation performance.', 'Core Technical', 'Suggested advanced undergraduate / postgraduate', 'Active'), ('COURSE_032', 'Water Resources Engineering', 'Civil Engineering', 'Civil Engineering', 'Water Resources Engineering examines the analysis, planning, and management of surface-water and groundwater resources. It covers hydrologic processes, measurement and data analysis, hydrologic modelling, reservoirs and storage, groundwater resources, water-resources systems, and flood and drought management, linking physical water processes with engineering decisions under variability and uncertainty.', 'Develop quantitative understanding of hydrologic systems and the ability to use observations and models for water-resources planning and design. Emphasise storage, allocation, groundwater interaction, extremes, uncertainty, and system-level decision making.', 'Analyse precipitation, infiltration, evaporation, runoff, and watershed processes; interpret hydrologic measurements; formulate and evaluate hydrologic models; analyse reservoir storage and operation; assess groundwater resources; evaluate water-resources systems; and develop engineering responses to floods and droughts.', 'Core Technical', 'Suggested advanced undergraduate / postgraduate', 'Active'), ('COURSE_033', 'Hydraulics and Hydraulic Engineering', 'Civil Engineering', 'Civil Engineering', 'Hydraulics and Hydraulic Engineering develops the mechanics and design methods used to analyse flowing and static fluids in engineering systems. It covers pressure and hydrostatics, Bernoulli-based energy analysis, pipe and open-channel flow, hydraulic machines, hydraulic structures, transient phenomena such as water hammer, and the integration of components into hydraulic systems.', 'Develop the ability to formulate hydraulic problems from conservation laws and constitutive relations, select appropriate flow models, size and evaluate hydraulic components, and assess transient and system-level behaviour.', 'Apply pressure, continuity, momentum, and energy principles to hydraulic systems; analyse pipe and open-channel flows; evaluate pumps and hydraulic machines; analyse hydraulic structures; assess transient flow and water hammer; and design integrated hydraulic systems subject to performance and operating constraints.', 'Core Technical', 'Suggested advanced undergraduate / postgraduate', 'Active'), ('COURSE_034', 'Engineering Geology', 'Civil Engineering', 'Civil Engineering', 'Engineering Geology applies geological knowledge to the investigation, design, construction, and risk assessment of infrastructure. It covers minerals and rocks, weathering and geological processes, geological structures and discontinuities, site characterisation, geological hazards, foundation and excavation conditions, and the interpretation of geological information for infrastructure projects.', 'Develop the ability to interpret geological materials and structures in engineering terms, recognise ground-related hazards, and translate geological observations into site and design constraints.', 'Identify and classify engineeringly significant minerals and rocks; interpret weathering and geological processes; characterise discontinuities and site conditions; assess geological hazards; evaluate implications for foundations and excavations; and incorporate geological evidence into infrastructure planning and design.', 'Core Technical', 'Suggested advanced undergraduate / postgraduate', 'Active'), ('COURSE_035', 'CAD/CAM', 'Mechanical Engineering', 'Mechanical Engineering', 'CAD/CAM integrates digital product definition with computer-aided manufacturing. The course covers geometric and solid modelling, parametric design, engineering graphics, assemblies and design intent, manufacturing planning, CNC programming and machining, computer-aided manufacturing, and the product-data and process links required to move from a digital design to manufacturable output.', 'Develop competence in creating and interrogating digital product models and using them to support manufacturing planning and CNC production. Emphasise geometric validity, design intent, manufacturability, process planning, and data continuity between design and manufacturing systems.', 'Create and evaluate geometric and parametric models; define assemblies and design intent; prepare manufacturing plans; develop CNC programs and machining strategies; use CAM systems for process generation; and evaluate CAD/CAM integration and product-data exchange for manufacturability.', 'Core Technical', 'Suggested advanced undergraduate / postgraduate', 'Active'), ('COURSE_036', 'Mechatronics', 'Mechanical Engineering', 'Mechanical Engineering', 'Mechatronics integrates mechanical systems with sensing, actuation, embedded computation, signal acquisition, and feedback control. The course covers sensors and transducers, motors and drives, embedded controllers, signal conditioning and data acquisition, system modelling and control, mechatronic integration, testing, and reliability.', 'Develop systems-level competence in selecting and integrating sensors, actuators, controllers, and interfaces. Emphasise signal integrity, closed-loop behaviour, embedded implementation, subsystem interactions, testing, and reliability.', 'Select sensors and actuators for specified requirements; condition and acquire measurement signals; implement embedded control functions; model and analyse mechatronic subsystems; integrate sensing, actuation, computation, and mechanics; and verify system performance and reliability through structured testing.', 'Core Technical', 'Suggested advanced undergraduate / postgraduate', 'Active'), ('COURSE_037', 'Refrigeration and Air Conditioning', 'Mechanical Engineering', 'Mechanical Engineering', 'Refrigeration and Air Conditioning applies thermodynamics and heat-transfer principles to the analysis and design of refrigeration and comfort-conditioning systems. It covers vapour-compression and related cycles, refrigerant properties and environmental considerations, compressors and other components, psychrometrics, cooling-load estimation, system configurations, performance metrics, and diagnostics.', 'Develop the ability to analyse refrigeration cycles and air-conditioning processes, select and size major components, estimate loads, evaluate system performance, and interpret refrigerant and psychrometric behaviour.', 'Analyse refrigeration cycles using thermodynamic property relations; compare refrigerants and account for safety and environmental constraints; evaluate compressors, condensers, expansion devices, and evaporators; perform psychrometric analysis and cooling-load calculations; and diagnose system performance and faults.', 'Core Technical', 'Suggested advanced undergraduate / postgraduate', 'Active'), ('COURSE_038', 'Internal Combustion Engines', 'Mechanical Engineering', 'Mechanical Engineering', 'Internal Combustion Engines examines the thermodynamic cycles, mechanical components, air and fuel systems, combustion processes, performance, emissions, after-treatment, and control strategies of reciprocating engines. The course links cycle analysis with real engine behaviour, component constraints, combustion efficiency, pollutant formation, and diagnostic measurements.', 'Develop the ability to analyse engine cycles and performance, interpret combustion and emissions behaviour, and evaluate the interaction of mechanical, fuel, air, exhaust, and control subsystems.', 'Analyse Otto, Diesel, and dual-cycle behaviour; explain the function and loading of major engine components; evaluate air and fuel systems; relate combustion processes to efficiency and emissions; interpret performance tests; evaluate after-treatment technologies; and use control and diagnostic information to assess engine operation.', 'Core Technical', 'Suggested advanced undergraduate / postgraduate', 'Active'), ('COURSE_039', 'Engineering Materials', 'Mechanical Engineering', 'Mechanical Engineering', 'Engineering Materials examines the structure, processing, properties, selection, and failure of materials used in engineering systems. It covers crystalline structure and defects, diffusion and phase transformations, ferrous and non-ferrous alloys, polymers and ceramics, composites, processing routes, material-property relationships, selection criteria, and failure mechanisms.', 'Develop the ability to relate material structure and processing history to mechanical, thermal, electrical, and chemical behaviour, and to select materials for specified service conditions.', 'Explain structure-property relationships and common material defects; analyse ferrous and non-ferrous alloys; compare polymers, ceramics, and composites; relate processing to microstructure and properties; select materials using performance and service requirements; and diagnose common material failure mechanisms.', 'Core Technical', 'Suggested advanced undergraduate / postgraduate', 'Active'), ('COURSE_040', 'Chemical Process Calculations', 'Chemical Engineering', 'Chemical Engineering', 'Chemical Process Calculations develops the quantitative methods used to formulate and solve material and energy balances for chemical-process systems. It covers units and dimensions, process variables, component and total balances, reactive systems, energy balances, combined balances, and process-flow calculations, with emphasis on consistent bases, assumptions, degrees of freedom, and physically meaningful solutions.', 'Develop rigorous balance-solving skills for steady-state and selected unsteady process calculations. Emphasise dimensional consistency, basis selection, independent equations, reaction stoichiometry, thermodynamic property data, and verification of results.', 'Set up dimensionally consistent process calculations; formulate total and component material balances; solve balances involving chemical reactions; perform energy balances; combine material and energy constraints; analyse process flowsheets; and check solutions against conservation laws and physical limits.', 'Core Technical', 'Suggested advanced undergraduate / postgraduate', 'Active'), ('COURSE_041', 'Fluid Flow and Heat Transfer', 'Chemical Engineering', 'Chemical Engineering', 'Fluid Flow and Heat Transfer integrates momentum and energy transport for engineering systems. It covers fluid properties and flow fundamentals, internal and external flow, boundary layers, dimensional and momentum analysis, heat conduction and convection, heat exchangers, radiation, and coupled thermal systems.', 'Develop the ability to formulate fluid-flow and heat-transfer problems using conservation laws, constitutive relations, and dimensionless analysis, and to select appropriate models for engineering design.', 'Analyse internal and external flows; evaluate pressure losses and boundary-layer behaviour; use momentum and dimensional analysis; solve conduction and convection problems; analyse and design heat exchangers; evaluate radiative transfer; and assess coupled fluid-thermal systems.', 'Core Technical', 'Suggested advanced undergraduate / postgraduate', 'Active'), ('COURSE_042', 'Mass Transfer Operations', 'Chemical Engineering', 'Chemical Engineering', 'Mass Transfer Operations develops the transport and separation principles used in chemical-process equipment. It covers molecular diffusion, interfacial mass transfer, gas absorption and stripping, distillation, liquid-liquid extraction, humidification and drying, adsorption and membrane separation, and the design and evaluation of mass-transfer equipment.', 'Develop competence in selecting and analysing separation operations from phase-equilibrium, mass-transfer, and process requirements. Emphasise operating lines, transfer coefficients, stagewise and differential contact, equipment choice, and design trade-offs.', 'Apply diffusion and mass-transfer relations; analyse absorption, stripping, and distillation systems; evaluate liquid-liquid extraction, humidification, and drying; compare adsorption and membrane separations; determine stage or transfer-unit requirements; and develop preliminary equipment designs.', 'Core Technical', 'Suggested advanced undergraduate / postgraduate', 'Active'), ('COURSE_043', 'Chemical Reaction Engineering', 'Chemical Engineering', 'Chemical Engineering', 'Chemical Reaction Engineering develops the kinetic and reactor-analysis framework used to convert reaction chemistry into process design. It covers rate laws and parameter estimation, ideal batch/CSTR/PFR models, multiple reactions and selectivity, non-isothermal operation, residence-time distributions, heterogeneous reactions, and reactor design and scale-up.', 'Develop the ability to connect reaction kinetics with reactor performance and design. Emphasise residence time, conversion, selectivity, heat effects, transport limitations, parameter estimation, and scale-up constraints.', 'Estimate and interpret kinetic parameters; analyse ideal batch, CSTR, and PFR behaviour; evaluate conversion and selectivity for multiple reactions; analyse non-isothermal reactors; use residence-time information to assess performance; account for heterogeneous effects; and develop reactor design and scale-up calculations.', 'Core Technical', 'Suggested advanced undergraduate / postgraduate', 'Active'), ('COURSE_044', 'Process Control and Instrumentation', 'Chemical Engineering', 'Chemical Engineering', 'Process Control and Instrumentation integrates measurement, final control, process dynamics, feedback control, controller tuning, advanced control, and industrial control architecture. It distinguishes the measurement chain from control computation and final actuation, and connects control-loop analysis with PLC, DCS, SCADA, and safety-related industrial systems.', 'Develop the ability to specify and evaluate industrial measurement and control loops, analyse dynamic behaviour, tune controllers, and select control architectures appropriate to process requirements and operational constraints.', 'Select and evaluate process measurements and final control elements; formulate process dynamic models; analyse feedback-loop behaviour; tune PID controllers; evaluate advanced control approaches; and distinguish the roles of PLC, DCS, SCADA, and safety-instrumented functions in industrial automation.', 'Core Technical', 'Suggested advanced undergraduate / postgraduate', 'Active'), ('COURSE_045', 'Electrical Machines', 'Electrical Engineering', 'Electrical Engineering', 'Electrical Machines develops the electromagnetic and electromechanical principles used to analyse transformers and rotating electrical machines. It covers magnetic circuits, induced EMF and torque production, transformers, DC machines, induction machines, synchronous machines, performance testing, losses, efficiency, and machine applications and drives.', 'Develop the ability to derive and use machine models, interpret operating characteristics, evaluate losses and efficiency, and select machines for specified electrical and mechanical duties.', 'Analyse magnetic circuits and electromechanical energy conversion; derive and use transformer and machine equivalent models; evaluate DC, induction, and synchronous machines; interpret performance and test results; quantify losses and efficiency; and select machines for applications and drive systems.', 'Core Technical', 'Suggested advanced undergraduate / postgraduate', 'Active'), ('COURSE_046', 'Power Systems', 'Electrical Engineering', 'Electrical Engineering', 'Power Systems examines the generation, transmission, distribution, protection, operation, and stability of electric-power networks. It covers system components and models, transmission and distribution, per-unit representation, power-flow analysis, fault calculations, protection, stability, operational control, and emerging grid architectures.', 'Develop competence in modelling power networks and evaluating steady-state, fault, protection, and dynamic behaviour. Emphasise system security, reliability, voltage and frequency constraints, and the changing architecture of modern grids.', 'Build network models and use the per-unit system; perform power-flow studies; analyse symmetrical and selected unsymmetrical faults; explain protection-system principles; assess transient and steady-state stability; and evaluate operational and emerging-grid requirements.', 'Core Technical', 'Suggested advanced undergraduate / postgraduate', 'Active'), ('COURSE_047', 'Power Electronics', 'Electrical Engineering', 'Electrical Engineering', 'Power Electronics studies the controlled conversion and conditioning of electrical power using semiconductor switches and converter topologies. It covers power devices, rectifiers, DC-DC converters, inverters, PWM and switching control, commutation, power quality, thermal constraints, and applications such as drives and power supplies.', 'Develop the ability to select converter topologies, analyse switching waveforms and device stresses, design basic control strategies, and evaluate efficiency, power quality, thermal behaviour, and application constraints.', 'Explain the operation and limitations of major power semiconductor devices; analyse rectifier, DC-DC, and inverter topologies; evaluate PWM and switching strategies; assess device losses, thermal constraints, and power quality; and select converter architectures for practical applications.', 'Core Technical', 'Suggested advanced undergraduate / postgraduate', 'Active'), ('COURSE_048', 'Electrical Measurements and Instrumentation', 'Electrical Engineering', 'Electrical Engineering', 'Electrical Measurements and Instrumentation develops the principles and practices used to acquire trustworthy electrical measurements. It covers measurement errors and performance characteristics, electrical instruments, sensors and transducers, signal conditioning and data acquisition, calibration, uncertainty and statistical evaluation, and instrumentation applications.', 'Develop competence in selecting measurement systems, interpreting instrument specifications, designing signal-conditioning chains, evaluating calibration and traceability, and quantifying uncertainty rather than reporting measurements without an uncertainty basis.', 'Distinguish accuracy, precision, resolution, and major error mechanisms; select and apply electrical instruments and transducers; design signal-conditioning and data-acquisition paths; interpret calibration results; estimate measurement uncertainty statistically; and evaluate instrumentation systems for specified applications.', 'Core Technical', 'Suggested advanced undergraduate / postgraduate', 'Active'), ('COURSE_049', 'Signals and Systems', 'Electrical Engineering', 'Electrical Engineering', 'Signals and Systems develops mathematical methods for representing and analysing continuous- and discrete-time signals and linear systems. It covers signal transformations, energy and power, linear time-invariant systems, convolution, Fourier analysis, Laplace transforms, Z transforms, sampling and reconstruction, and system characterisation.', 'Develop the ability to move between time- and transform-domain representations, derive system responses, and interpret the implications of linearity, time invariance, stability, frequency content, and sampling.', 'Represent and classify signals; analyse LTI systems using convolution and impulse responses; use Fourier, Laplace, and Z-transform methods; analyse discrete-time systems; evaluate sampling and reconstruction; and characterise systems in time and frequency domains.', 'Core Technical', 'Suggested advanced undergraduate / postgraduate', 'Active'), ('COURSE_050', 'Environmental Law and Environmental Governance', 'Law', 'Law', 'Environmental Law and Environmental Governance examines the legal principles, institutions, regulatory mechanisms, liability regimes, adjudicatory processes, and governance practices used to prevent and manage environmental harm. It covers environmental rights and principles, regulatory authority, pollution control, liability and enforcement, natural-resource governance, environmental adjudication, and institutional implementation.', 'Develop the ability to identify applicable legal principles and institutional responsibilities, analyse regulatory decisions and liability, and evaluate how environmental governance mechanisms operate in practice. Jurisdiction-specific rules should be supplied and validated separately when the repository is deployed for a particular legal system.', 'Explain major environmental-law principles and institutional arrangements; analyse regulatory and pollution-control mechanisms; assess liability and enforcement issues; evaluate natural-resource governance; interpret environmental adjudication and remedies; and critically assess governance arrangements in context.', 'Core Technical', 'Suggested advanced undergraduate / postgraduate', 'Active'), ('COURSE_051', 'Environmental Impact Assessment Law', 'Law', 'Law', 'Environmental Impact Assessment Law examines the legal architecture governing environmental assessment of proposed projects and plans. It covers statutory and regulatory bases, screening, scoping and terms of reference, impact assessment and appraisal, public participation, environmental decision making, post-approval compliance and monitoring, litigation, and comparative approaches.', 'Develop the ability to trace an EIA requirement through its legal basis, procedural stages, decision points, participation requirements, compliance obligations, and available remedies. Jurisdiction-specific legal rules should be attached as validated evidence rather than assumed in the core ontology.', 'Identify the legal basis and scope of EIA requirements; distinguish screening, scoping, assessment, appraisal, and decision stages; analyse participation and consultation requirements; evaluate compliance and monitoring obligations; and examine litigation and comparative approaches to EIA governance.', 'Core Technical', 'Suggested advanced undergraduate / postgraduate', 'Active'), ('COURSE_052', 'Climate Change Law and Policy', 'Law', 'Law', 'Climate Change Law and Policy examines the legal and policy instruments used to govern mitigation, adaptation, climate finance, disclosure, accountability, and implementation. It connects domestic governance with international climate-law frameworks and considers how emissions regulation, resilience, finance, reporting, and litigation interact in policy implementation.', 'Develop the ability to distinguish legal obligations from policy commitments, analyse climate-governance instruments, and evaluate implementation and accountability mechanisms. Jurisdiction-specific legislation should be introduced only through validated legal sources.', 'Explain major climate-governance and legal frameworks; compare mitigation and adaptation instruments; analyse climate finance and disclosure mechanisms; interpret international climate-law arrangements; examine climate litigation and accountability; and evaluate implementation challenges across sectors and institutions.', 'Core Technical', 'Suggested advanced undergraduate / postgraduate', 'Active'), ('COURSE_053', 'Contract Law and Commercial Agreements', 'Law', 'Law', 'Contract Law and Commercial Agreements develops the legal reasoning required to form, interpret, perform, manage, and resolve disputes arising from contracts. It covers formation, terms and interpretation, performance and discharge, breach and remedies, commercial risk allocation, drafting and negotiation, and common structures used in commercial agreements.', 'Develop the ability to identify contractual obligations and risk allocation, interpret contractual terms, assess performance and breach, and translate commercial requirements into legally coherent drafting and negotiation positions. Applicable jurisdictional rules should be validated separately.', 'Analyse formation and enforceability issues; interpret express and implied terms; assess performance, discharge, breach, and remedies; evaluate contractual allocation of commercial risk; identify drafting and negotiation issues; and analyse common commercial-agreement structures.', 'Core Technical', 'Suggested advanced undergraduate / postgraduate', 'Active'), ('COURSE_054', 'Labour and Employment Law', 'Law', 'Law', 'Labour and Employment Law examines the legal regulation of employment relationships, working conditions, wages and benefits, industrial relations, workplace regulation, equality and workplace rights, dispute resolution, and contemporary employment issues. The course distinguishes individual employment obligations from collective labour relations and regulatory enforcement.', 'Develop the ability to identify employment-law issues, interpret workplace rights and obligations, analyse collective and individual disputes, and evaluate regulatory compliance. Jurisdiction-specific statutes and case law should be linked through validated legal sources.', 'Analyse employment relationships and conditions; evaluate wage, benefit, and workplace obligations; distinguish collective and individual labour relations; assess occupational and workplace regulation; analyse equality and workplace-rights issues; and evaluate dispute-resolution and adjudication mechanisms.', 'Core Technical', 'Suggested advanced undergraduate / postgraduate', 'Active'), ('COURSE_055', 'Corporate, Sustainability and ESG Law', 'Law', 'Law', 'Corporate, Sustainability and ESG Law examines how corporate governance, directors'' duties, environmental and social responsibilities, disclosure, sustainable finance, and corporate risk are translated into legal obligations and accountability mechanisms. It treats ESG as a governance and legal domain rather than as a generic sustainability label.', 'Develop the ability to analyse corporate decision-making and accountability through governance, fiduciary, disclosure, sustainability, and risk lenses. Applicable corporate and securities-law requirements should be jurisdiction-specific and evidence-backed when implemented.', 'Explain corporate governance structures and directors'' duties; analyse environmental and social responsibilities; evaluate ESG disclosure and reporting obligations; assess sustainable-finance and corporate-risk issues; examine compliance and enforcement; and analyse ESG-related legal disputes and accountability mechanisms.', 'Core Technical', 'Suggested advanced undergraduate / postgraduate', 'Active'), ('COURSE_056', 'Sociology', 'Social Science', 'Social Science', 'Sociology examines how social structures, institutions, culture, interaction, groups, organisations, inequality, population processes, and social change shape collective life. It introduces major sociological theories and applies them to empirical questions involving stratification, institutions, urbanisation, networks, and contemporary social transformations.', 'Develop sociological reasoning that distinguishes individual-level behaviour from structural and institutional processes. Emphasise theory-informed interpretation, evidence, comparative analysis, and awareness of how social context shapes observed outcomes.', 'Explain major sociological perspectives; analyse social institutions and processes of socialisation; examine culture, stratification, and inequality; analyse groups, organisations, and networks; interpret population and urbanisation patterns; and apply sociological theory to contemporary social change.', 'Core Technical', 'Suggested advanced undergraduate / postgraduate', 'Active'), ('COURSE_057', 'Social Psychology', 'Social Science', 'Social Science', 'Social Psychology examines how people perceive, interpret, and influence one another and how social contexts shape cognition, attitudes, identity, behaviour, and group processes. It covers social cognition, attribution, attitudes and persuasion, interpersonal processes, groups and collective behaviour, identity and intergroup relations, social influence, communication, and applied social-psychological analysis.', 'Develop the ability to distinguish individual cognitive processes from interpersonal and group-level mechanisms and to interpret empirical evidence about social behaviour. Emphasise theory, experimental and correlational evidence, measurement, and contextual interpretation.', 'Explain core models of social cognition and attribution; analyse attitude formation and persuasion; evaluate interpersonal and group processes; examine identity and intergroup relations; analyse social influence and communication; and apply social-psychological theory to real-world problems.', 'Core Technical', 'Suggested advanced undergraduate / postgraduate', 'Active'), ('COURSE_058', 'Development Economics', 'Social Science', 'Social Science', 'Development Economics examines economic processes associated with growth, structural transformation, poverty reduction, inequality, human capital, labour, institutions, markets, finance, public policy, and international integration. It combines economic models with empirical evidence to explain why development outcomes differ across people, regions, and countries.', 'Develop the ability to analyse development outcomes using economic theory and empirical evidence, while recognising institutional, distributional, and historical factors. Emphasise measurement, causal reasoning, policy evaluation, and trade-offs rather than treating development as a single growth metric.', 'Analyse growth and structural transformation; measure and interpret poverty and inequality; evaluate human-capital and labour-market mechanisms; examine institutions and development; analyse markets and development finance; evaluate development policies; and interpret international integration and development outcomes.', 'Core Technical', 'Suggested advanced undergraduate / postgraduate', 'Active'), ('COURSE_059', 'Public Policy and Governance', 'Social Science', 'Social Science', 'Public Policy and Governance examines how public problems are defined, placed on policy agendas, translated into instruments, implemented through institutions, regulated, financed, and evaluated. It covers policy actors and institutions, formulation, implementation, governance and regulation, public finance, evaluation, digital governance, and contemporary policy challenges.', 'Develop the ability to trace policy from problem definition through formulation, implementation, and evaluation, while analysing institutional capacity, stakeholder interests, regulatory choices, resource constraints, and evidence.', 'Define and frame policy problems; analyse institutions and policy actors; evaluate policy-formulation alternatives; assess implementation mechanisms and institutional capacity; analyse governance and regulation; examine public-finance constraints; design evaluation approaches; and assess digital-governance developments.', 'Core Technical', 'Suggested advanced undergraduate / postgraduate', 'Active'), ('COURSE_060', 'Research Methods in Social Sciences', 'Social Science', 'Social Science', 'Research Methods in Social Sciences develops the methodological foundations for designing, conducting, analysing, and reporting empirical social-science research. It covers research questions and designs, sampling, measurement and instruments, qualitative methods, quantitative analysis, mixed-methods integration, validity and reliability, research quality, ethics, and research integrity.', 'Develop the ability to align research questions, theory, design, sampling, measurement, data collection, analysis, and inference. Emphasise methodological fit, transparency, validity, reliability, ethical conduct, and reproducibility.', 'Formulate researchable questions and hypotheses; select defensible research designs and sampling strategies; design and evaluate measurement instruments; apply qualitative and quantitative methods; integrate mixed-method evidence; assess validity and reliability; and conduct and report research in accordance with ethical and integrity requirements.', 'Core Technical', 'Suggested advanced undergraduate / postgraduate', 'Active')') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;